ted in Muchowska et al. (2019, Nature) and Ritson (2021, Sci Adv)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Formaldehyde Cannizzaro disproportionation to glycolic acid", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Basic hydrothermal, surface of vents"}, "mechanism": "Crossed Cannizzaro/aldol\u2014one formaldehyde oxidized, other reduced, condense to C2 acid.", "reasoning": "Supported under simulated vent conditions; see Aubrey et al. (2008, Origins Life Evol. Biosph.)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Acetate UV photoredox coupling to glycolic acid", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ultraviolet radiation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "UV", "aqueous": "true", "other": "Shallow UV-exposed pool, wet-dry cycle"}, "mechanism": "UV radical formation couples acetate with water-derived species, forms glycolic acid.", "reasoning": "UV-driven route validated by Liu et al. (2021, Nature Chem)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Cyanosulfidic surface aminonitrile hydrolysis", "description": "Aminoacetonitrile forms under HCN-rich surface pools, then hydrolyzes to glycine during aqueous wet phases.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_002"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Iron-catalyzed hydrothermal reductive amination of glyoxylate", "description": "Hydrothermal glyoxylate reacts with hydroxylamine in the presence of iron to furnish glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Hydrothermal peracetic acid amination", "description": "Peracetic acid generated from acetate oxidizes, then reacts with ammonia to make glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Serine retro-aldol decomposition", "description": "L-Serine undergoes retro-aldol cleavage in hot hydrothermal waters, yielding glycine and formaldehyde.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "L-Threonine direct thermal decomposition to glycine", "description": "Hot hydrothermal conditions decarboxylate threonine directly to glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "L-Threonine decomposition to glycine and ethylene oxide", "description": "Alternative threonine breakdown route; forms glycine and ethylene oxide.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "L-Asparagine hydrolytic cleavage to two glycine", "description": "Hydrothermal decomposition of L-asparagine yields two equivalents of glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Hydrothermal isocitrate conversion via glyoxylate intermediate", "description": "Isocitrate iron-catalyzed cleavage forms glyoxylate, which then undergoes reductive amination to glycine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008", "rxn_002"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Surface UV-driven formation of glyoxylate from 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid, entry to hydrothermal glycine routes", "description": "UV-photochemistry forms glyoxylate from 2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid; glyoxylate then converted to glycine hydrothermally.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_002"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Hydrothermal and surface glycolic acid-to-glycine axis", "description": "Glycolic acid formed from either formaldehyde (hydrothermal Cannizzaro), acetate (UV), or glyoxylate reduction, which channels metabolic flux to glycine via oxidation/reductive amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_010", "r